1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:15,007
Catherine Adeyemi's expansion plan was eleven pages
long and had taken her three months,

2
00:00:15,027 --> 00:00:18,949
and it was, by any ordinary
measure, aggressive.

3
00:00:19,469 --> 00:00:24,246
Four regional headquarters. London for Europe and the
regulators, Singapore for Asia and

4
00:00:24,266 --> 00:00:28,879
manufacturing, Zurich for the Swiss pharmaceutical
relationships and the money, Tokyo

5
00:00:28,899 --> 00:00:33,567
because our clinical partners are there and because
we cannot keep flying people eight

6
00:00:33,587 --> 00:00:35,638
thousand miles to be polite in person.

7
00:00:35,918 --> 00:00:41,447
Build-out over five years. Leased floors in year one,
purpose-built by 2034. Nine hundred

8
00:00:41,467 --> 00:00:47,058
million dollars of capital expenditure, phased. It is
the fastest I can make it go without

9
00:00:47,078 --> 00:00:48,242
breaking something.

10
00:00:48,643 --> 00:00:49,832
Why buildings?

11
00:00:50,232 --> 00:00:51,642
I beg your pardon?

12
00:00:52,042 --> 00:00:57,115
You have just described four leases, three thousand
employees and a great deal of travel.

13
00:00:57,135 --> 00:00:59,232
Why does any of that require a tower?

14
00:00:59,633 --> 00:01:04,650
Because a company that looks like a laboratory in
Delaware cannot negotiate with the Japanese

15
00:01:04,670 --> 00:01:09,417
Ministry of Health. Because a regulator in Singapore
will not licence a plant for a firm

16
00:01:09,437 --> 00:01:14,129
with no address in the country. Because when the
Swiss decide whether you are a peer or

17
00:01:14,149 --> 00:01:17,108
a supplier, they decide it by
looking at your building.

18
00:01:17,509 --> 00:01:19,539
Wesker considered that.

19
00:01:20,058 --> 00:01:24,887
You are right, and you have under-asked. Take the
plan, and remove every word that says

20
00:01:24,907 --> 00:01:25,389
*phased*.

21
00:01:25,789 --> 00:01:30,816
That's four purpose-built towers in year one. That is
not possible. There is no construction

22
00:01:30,836 --> 00:01:32,188
schedule on this planet —

23
00:01:32,587 --> 00:01:37,826
Ms. Adeyemi. In the eighteen months you have worked
here, how many times have you told

24
00:01:37,846 --> 00:01:39,537
me a thing was not possible?

25
00:01:40,212 --> 00:01:41,320
(flat) Six.

26
00:01:41,721 --> 00:01:44,133
And how many times were you correct?

27
00:01:44,534 --> 00:01:50,235
She did not answer that. She went back to her office
and rewrote page nine, and at the

28
00:01:50,255 --> 00:01:55,890
bottom of the rewritten page she put a line that
had not been in any document she had

29
00:01:55,910 --> 00:02:01,678
ever produced in twenty-six years in London:
*Capital expenditure: to be advised by the

30
00:02:01,698 --> 00:02:02,343
Director.*

31
00:02:02,743 --> 00:02:09,263
The narrator notes that this is the precise moment
Catherine Adeyemi stopped being Umbrella's

32
00:02:09,283 --> 00:02:15,380
chief financial officer in the ordinary sense and
became something else: the person who

33
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:20,653
wrote down, in a form auditors could accept,
whatever had already happened.

34
00:02:21,804 --> 00:02:29,382
Regional Headquarters Tower. Forty-one floors, fitted
and staffed: nine hundred thousand

35
00:02:29,402 --> 00:02:36,722
each. Integration covers deeds, permits, planning
history, the skyline record and the

36
00:02:36,742 --> 00:02:44,062
photographic archive. Note: this item alters what
is remembered by approximately nine

37
00:02:44,082 --> 00:02:46,567
million people per placement.

38
00:02:47,157 --> 00:02:49,467
He read that last line four times.

39
00:02:49,986 --> 00:02:51,788
Nine million people.

40
00:02:52,189 --> 00:03:00,308
Approximately. A tower in a photographed skyline is not
a building in a field. Every postcard,

41
00:03:00,328 --> 00:03:08,100
every establishing shot, every tourist's holiday
album, every child's drawing of the city.

42
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:09,745
It is a large edit.

43
00:03:10,144 --> 00:03:12,334
And the cost of a large edit?

44
00:03:12,734 --> 00:03:20,088
To me, Points. To you, nothing. To them — a very
slight thinning. People whose memories

45
00:03:20,108 --> 00:03:27,293
have been extensively revised become marginally
less curious. It is not damage. It is

46
00:03:27,313 --> 00:03:31,616
the way a much-folded map goes
soft at the creases.

47
00:03:32,457 --> 00:03:38,331
The narrator asks you to notice that Albert Wesker
asked that question, and that he got

48
00:03:38,351 --> 00:03:43,953
an honest answer, and that he then spent three
million six hundred thousand Points.

49
00:03:44,473 --> 00:03:49,163
Four towers. London, Singapore, Zurich,
Tokyo. Place them this weekend.

50
00:03:49,563 --> 00:03:50,878
You hesitated.

51
00:03:51,277 --> 00:03:52,407
I priced it.

52
00:03:52,807 --> 00:03:55,723
That is not the same thing as not caring.

53
00:03:56,123 --> 00:04:00,653
No. It is better. A man who does not care
makes mistakes with the currency.

54
00:04:01,054 --> 00:04:06,705
The narrator will describe one of the four, because
all four went the same way and one

55
00:04:06,725 --> 00:04:08,683
is enough to break your heart.

56
00:04:09,084 --> 00:04:14,881
On the south bank of the Thames, between Blackfriars
and Southwark, there is a plot that

57
00:04:14,901 --> 00:04:20,567
had been a car park, then a hoarding with an
artist's impression on it, then a hole in

58
00:04:20,587 --> 00:04:25,724
the ground with a stalled financing and a crane
that had not moved since 2024.

59
00:04:26,124 --> 00:04:32,488
On Saturday morning there was a tower. Forty-one
floors of grey glass with a crimson iris

60
00:04:32,508 --> 00:04:38,801
at its base, finished in 2019, designed by a practice
that had won an award for it, with

61
00:04:38,821 --> 00:04:44,971
a public plaza, a coffee franchise on the ground
floor and twenty-six years of weather

62
00:04:44,991 --> 00:04:45,904
on the stone.

63
00:04:46,303 --> 00:04:52,173
The crane was gone. The hoarding was gone. The
stalled financing had never existed.

64
00:04:52,574 --> 00:04:54,634
And this is the part that matters.

65
00:04:55,033 --> 00:05:01,356
In eleven million photographs — in every panorama of
the London skyline taken from Waterloo

66
00:05:01,376 --> 00:05:07,630
Bridge since 2019, in every establishing shot in every
television drama, in the background

67
00:05:07,650 --> 00:05:13,554
of a wedding, in a screensaver, in a school
geography textbook, in a painting hanging

68
00:05:13,574 --> 00:05:18,434
in a pub in Battersea — the tower was there.
It had always been there.

69
00:05:18,834 --> 00:05:24,456
A photographer named Ines Okoro, who had shot that
exact view every New Year's Day for

70
00:05:24,476 --> 00:05:30,624
nine years as a personal project, sat at her kitchen
table on the Sunday with nine photographs

71
00:05:30,644 --> 00:05:35,874
in front of her and a tower in all of them, and
she put her hand over her mouth.

72
00:05:36,274 --> 00:05:42,043
She knew. For about forty seconds, Ines Okoro knew,
completely and without any doubt at

73
00:05:42,063 --> 00:05:45,704
all, that something enormous had
happened to the world.

74
00:05:46,103 --> 00:05:51,631
And then the doubt came in — not as an argument, but
as a softness, a kind of gentle fog

75
00:05:51,651 --> 00:05:56,990
at the edge of the thought — and by Monday she had
decided she must simply never have

76
00:05:57,010 --> 00:06:02,664
noticed it, because how could you not notice a tower,
and therefore she had always noticed

77
00:06:02,684 --> 00:06:05,564
it, and therefore there was
nothing to notice.

78
00:06:05,963 --> 00:06:11,188
She took the photograph again the following New
Year. She never looked at the old ones

79
00:06:11,208 --> 00:06:11,554
again.

80
00:06:11,954 --> 00:06:17,755
The System had called it *a very slight thinning*.
This is what that means, one person

81
00:06:17,775 --> 00:06:21,884
at a time, nine million at a stroke,
four times in a weekend.

82
00:06:22,283 --> 00:06:26,973
There was one man who did not let it go, and
his profession is the reason.

83
00:06:27,374 --> 00:06:33,359
A credit officer at a Zurich private bank found, in
his own institution's loan book, a

84
00:06:33,379 --> 00:06:39,433
facility of four hundred and eleven million francs
secured on a tower two streets away,

85
00:06:39,453 --> 00:06:45,438
drawn in 2018, performing, never once in arrears.
His signature was on the third page.

86
00:06:45,458 --> 00:06:51,374
He could remember the negotiation. He could
remember disliking the borrower's lawyer.

87
00:06:51,774 --> 00:06:53,364
He could not find the file.

88
00:06:53,764 --> 00:06:59,353
He spent eleven days on it. He went to the archive
in person. He pulled the microfilm.

89
00:06:59,373 --> 00:07:05,027
The microfilm had it. He pulled the committee
minutes. The minutes had it. He asked the

90
00:07:05,047 --> 00:07:10,571
two colleagues who had sat on that committee and
both remembered the deal, and one of

91
00:07:10,591 --> 00:07:12,854
them remembered arguing against it.

92
00:07:13,254 --> 00:07:18,740
On the twelfth day he stopped, and the narrator
would like to be very clear about why,

93
00:07:18,760 --> 00:07:21,494
because it was not fear and it
was not fog.

94
00:07:21,894 --> 00:07:27,471
It was that the loan was *performing*. Every
quarter, on the day, four million francs

95
00:07:27,491 --> 00:07:33,134
of interest arrived from a company that was now the
ninth largest in the world. He had

96
00:07:33,154 --> 00:07:38,929
a file that was complete, a borrower that was
immaculate, and a question that could only

97
00:07:38,949 --> 00:07:39,653
damage him.

98
00:07:40,173 --> 00:07:44,958
What am I supposed to write? That the building is
real, the money is real, the signature

99
00:07:44,978 --> 00:07:48,343
is mine, and I would like an
investigation into my own memory?

100
00:07:48,744 --> 00:07:54,866
He closed the folder. He was promoted in 2031, and
he retired in 2044 to a house above

101
00:07:54,886 --> 00:08:01,080
Lake Thun, and once, drunk, at a wedding, he told
his son-in-law that he had once found

102
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:07,294
a tower that had never been built, and his
son-in-law thought it was a joke about Swiss

103
00:08:07,314 --> 00:08:08,793
banking, and laughed.

104
00:08:09,194 --> 00:08:16,014
Marcus Reese was in this story once, in 2027, as a
nineteen-year-old with a drone and

105
00:08:16,034 --> 00:08:21,004
twenty-four hundred photographs of a
riverfront in Wilmington.

106
00:08:21,403 --> 00:08:28,059
He was twenty-one in 2029, and living in London, and
on the Sunday morning after the towers

107
00:08:28,079 --> 00:08:34,367
he opened a folder of his own photographs of the
Thames skyline, and sat and looked at

108
00:08:34,387 --> 00:08:36,274
them for about four hours.

109
00:08:36,794 --> 00:08:41,576
I'd done this before. That's the thing. I'd been
through it in Delaware two years earlier

110
00:08:41,596 --> 00:08:46,270
and I'd talked myself out of it, and I'd promised
myself that if it ever happened again

111
00:08:46,290 --> 00:08:48,104
I would not talk myself out of it.

112
00:08:48,384 --> 00:08:53,446
So I sat there with nine photographs and a tower in
all of them, and I did not talk myself

113
00:08:53,466 --> 00:08:56,495
out of it, and it was the worst four
hours of my life.

114
00:08:56,894 --> 00:09:03,305
What he did next is the reason he matters. He did not
post. He did not go to a journalist,

115
00:09:03,325 --> 00:09:05,235
or a forum, or a professor.

116
00:09:05,634 --> 00:09:11,148
He started a file. He photographed the skyline on the
same date every year, from the same

117
00:09:11,168 --> 00:09:16,868
place, and wrote down the weather. He collected other
people's — not screenshots; originals,

118
00:09:16,888 --> 00:09:22,464
with cameras, with metadata, bought where he had to
buy them. He built, over four decades,

119
00:09:22,484 --> 00:09:27,873
the largest independent photographic archive of the
built environment in the world, and

120
00:09:27,893 --> 00:09:30,485
he never once published a theory about it.

121
00:09:30,884 --> 00:09:37,946
When Albert Wesker published the catalogue in 2212,
Marcus Reese had been dead for ninety-one

122
00:09:37,966 --> 00:09:44,419
years, and his archive — nineteen million images,
willed to a university that had not

123
00:09:44,439 --> 00:09:51,882
wanted it and had accepted it out of embarrassment —
became the single most valuable corroborating

124
00:09:51,902 --> 00:09:54,394
source in the Assembly's inquiry.

125
00:09:54,794 --> 00:10:00,663
Four hundred and eleven placements were confirmed
from it, including nine that Umbrella's

126
00:10:00,683 --> 00:10:03,044
own ledger had recorded incorrectly.

127
00:10:03,445 --> 00:10:08,962
The narrator notes that there is a room named after
him in the Assembly's archive, that

128
00:10:08,982 --> 00:10:14,627
nobody in it ever met him, and that in his own
lifetime he was best known to eight people

129
00:10:14,647 --> 00:10:18,254
on a forum as a man who was slightly
odd about buildings.

130
00:10:18,654 --> 00:10:21,944
Three regulators opened files in
the first month.

131
00:10:22,345 --> 00:10:28,706
The Monetary Authority of Singapore queried the
ownership chain of the Marina Boulevard

132
00:10:28,726 --> 00:10:35,014
property and received, within nine days, a complete
conveyancing history going back to

133
00:10:35,034 --> 00:10:38,975
a reclamation grant in 2011, with
tax paid every year.

134
00:10:39,375 --> 00:10:45,309
A Tokyo ward office queried the seismic
certification of a forty-one-storey building it

135
00:10:45,329 --> 00:10:51,264
had no record of inspecting, and found its own
inspection reports, with the inspector's

136
00:10:51,284 --> 00:10:57,355
stamp, and the inspector — who was sixty-three and
had inspected eight thousand buildings

137
00:10:57,375 --> 00:11:02,215
— remembered the job because of an argument
about the sprinkler risers.

138
00:11:02,615 --> 00:11:08,244
The British planning authority did not query
anything, because in Britain the tower had

139
00:11:08,264 --> 00:11:14,023
won an award, and there is no institution on earth
less inclined to doubt a building than

140
00:11:14,043 --> 00:11:15,906
the one that gave it a prize.

141
00:11:17,057 --> 00:11:25,544
Expansion. Umbrella Life Sciences, present in sixty-one
countries. Headquarters in Wilmington,

142
00:11:25,564 --> 00:11:33,780
London, Singapore, Zurich and Tokyo. Fourteen thousand
nine hundred personnel, one thousand

143
00:11:33,800 --> 00:11:41,836
two hundred and forty of them Absolute. Nine hundred
thousand Points. Level thirty-eight.

144
00:11:43,054 --> 00:11:50,748
Main mission. Tier three: Agency. Sovereign-level
contracts in two nations. Operations

145
00:11:50,768 --> 00:11:58,462
on a second continent: complete. One hundred
thousand personnel. Reward: three million

146
00:11:58,482 --> 00:12:03,125
Points, the Intelligence tab, and
the Agency banner.

147
00:12:03,716 --> 00:12:09,151
He went to London in February, to the forty-first
floor of a building that had won an

148
00:12:09,171 --> 00:12:14,606
award in a year it did not exist, and stood at the
window over the river in the dark.

149
00:12:15,005 --> 00:12:17,135
The voice inside him, unasked.

150
00:12:17,655 --> 00:12:18,424
Say it.

151
00:12:18,825 --> 00:12:19,795
Say what?

152
00:12:20,195 --> 00:12:25,163
You have been standing there for twenty-three
minutes. You are not admiring the view;

153
00:12:25,183 --> 00:12:28,567
you have no interest in views. You are
counting something.

154
00:12:28,967 --> 00:12:31,377
Wesker did not turn around.

155
00:12:31,898 --> 00:12:36,332
Nine million people per tower. Thirty-six million
people have been quietly edited this

156
00:12:36,352 --> 00:12:40,786
month so that four buildings could stand in four
cities, and not one of them will ever

157
00:12:40,806 --> 00:12:45,240
know, and the only measurable effect is that they
will be very slightly less likely to

158
00:12:45,260 --> 00:12:47,467
ask a question for the rest of
their lives.

159
00:12:47,868 --> 00:12:49,314
And this troubles you.

160
00:12:49,714 --> 00:12:54,220
It *interests* me. I have spent two lifetimes making
people believe things. It required

161
00:12:54,240 --> 00:12:58,695
money, and pressure, and in the last case a great
deal of blood, and it never held for

162
00:12:58,715 --> 00:13:03,638
more than a few years, because a lie has to be
maintained. This does not have to be maintained.

163
00:13:03,658 --> 00:13:06,604
It is simply *true* now. The tower
has always been there.

164
00:13:07,004 --> 00:13:09,451
Then what is the difficulty?

165
00:13:09,850 --> 00:13:16,416
And here Albert Wesker turned around, and said the
sentence that is the single best description

166
00:13:16,436 --> 00:13:22,655
of him in this entire account, and that explains
everything he does for the next sixty-two

167
00:13:22,675 --> 00:13:23,071
years.

168
00:13:23,591 --> 00:13:28,151
The difficulty is that I did not do it. *It* did it.
I spent three million six hundred

169
00:13:28,171 --> 00:13:32,838
thousand Points and a machine I do not understand
reached into thirty-six million skulls

170
00:13:32,858 --> 00:13:37,525
on my behalf, and I have no idea what the exchange
rate is, or who is paying it, or what

171
00:13:37,545 --> 00:13:40,560
happens to a species that has been
folded too many times.

172
00:13:41,171 --> 00:13:45,441
I am not troubled by the cost. I am troubled
by not knowing what it is.

173
00:13:45,841 --> 00:13:47,326
Then stop spending.

174
00:13:47,725 --> 00:13:49,135
Don't be absurd.

175
00:13:49,535 --> 00:13:54,771
He turned back to the window, and the narrator would
like to point out that thirty-six

176
00:13:54,791 --> 00:14:00,150
million was the largest edit the System had yet made
on his behalf, and that within four

177
00:14:00,170 --> 00:14:05,405
years the number would be a billion, and that he
never did find out the exchange rate.

178
00:14:05,806 --> 00:14:07,996
He simply got better at not asking.
